.\"
.\" SHMAT(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH SHMAT 2 "June 2026" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
shmat, shmdt \- attach and detach a System V shared memory segment
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sys/ipc.h>
.B #include <sys/shm.h>
.PP
.BI "void *shmat(int " shmid ", const void *" shmaddr ", int " shmflg ");
.BI "int shmdt(const void *" shmaddr ");
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR shmat ()
attaches the shared memory segment identified by
.I shmid
to the address space of the calling process and returns the address at
which it is mapped.  Every process that attaches the same segment maps the
same physical pages, so a write by one attacher is immediately visible to
all the others (this is a genuinely shared mapping, not copy-on-write).
.PP
If
.I shmaddr
is NULL the kernel chooses a free, page-aligned address.  Otherwise the
segment is attached at
.IR shmaddr ,
which must be page-aligned unless
.B SHM_RND
is set in
.IR shmflg ,
in which case it is rounded down to the nearest multiple of
.BR SHMLBA .
.PP
The following flags may be ORed into
.IR shmflg :
.TP
.B SHM_RDONLY
Attach the segment read-only.  Without this flag the segment is attached
read/write (write permission is still required).
.TP
.B SHM_RND
Round
.I shmaddr
down to a
.B SHMLBA
boundary instead of requiring exact page alignment.
.TP
.B SHM_EXEC
Attach the segment with execute permission.
.PP
.BR shmdt ()
detaches the segment attached at
.I shmaddr
(which must be an address returned by a previous
.BR shmat ())
from the calling process.  When the last attachment of a segment that has
been marked for removal with
.BR shmctl (2)
.B IPC_RMID
is detached, the segment's backing pages are freed.  All of a process's
attachments are detached automatically when it exits.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success
.BR shmat ()
returns the address of the attached segment;
.BR shmdt ()
returns 0.  On error
.BR shmat ()
returns
.RB ( void
.BR * )\-1
and
.BR shmdt ()
returns \-1, with
.I errno
set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EACCES
The caller lacks the permission required for the requested attach mode.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I shmid
is not a valid identifier,
.I shmaddr
is misaligned (and
.B SHM_RND
was not given), or (for
.BR shmdt ())
no segment is attached at
.IR shmaddr .
.TP
.B EIDRM
The segment was removed.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
No free address-space region of the required size could be found.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shmget (2),
.BR shmctl (2)
